;; The module _hashlib (queue 537): Modules/_hashopenssl.c of CPython 3.14.10 over Go's crypto in place of OpenSSL's EVP
;; interface, as _ssl is over Go's crypto/tls (PYS22 (a)): hashlib.py and hmac.py take it first when it imports. Its
;; names: new(name, data=b'', *, usedforsecurity=True, string=None) and openssl_md5 ... openssl_shake_256 (the types
;; HASH and HASHXOF, a subclass of HASH: update, digest, hexdigest, copy; name, digest_size, block_size; repr
;; "<NAME _hashlib.HASH object @ ADDRESS>"; DISALLOW_INSTANTIATION), hmac_new(key, msg=b'', digestmod=None) (the type
;; HMAC, builtins_hmac.go's hmacObject with this module's type), hmac_digest(key, msg, digest), pbkdf2_hmac,
;; scrypt, compare_digest, get_fips_mode (0: no FIPS provider), openssl_md_meth_names, _constructors (a mappingproxy of
;; the openssl_* functions to their names), UnsupportedDigestmodError (a ValueError) and _GIL_MINSIZE. The digests are
;; the ones py_hashes names that Go's crypto has (md5, sha1, sha2 with the truncated sha512s, sha3, shake and blake2,
;; the last by hashlib_blake2.go); a name is found as py_digest_by_name finds it: the Python name, else OpenSSL's
;; names and aliases, which OpenSSL's fetch takes in any case. OpenSSL's further digests (md5-sha1, ripemd160, sm3)
;; are not offered: they are absent from openssl_md_meth_names, and new() refuses them as an unsupported digest. The
;; errors that OpenSSL's error queue words are given in its words: "[digital envelope routines] unsupported" for an
;; unknown digest, "no reason supplied" for an HMAC over an XOF, scrypt's "memory limit exceeded".


